 { Viktor Bologan – Baris Esen, Konya 2012 Learning objective: This game illustrates how White may combine his queenside expansion with kingside threats. } 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. Be3 e5 7. Nb3 Be6 8. f3 h5 9. Qd2 Nbd7 10. Nd5 Bxd5 11. exd5 { We reach the thematic structure with a standard theoretical position. There are over 150 games with this position, and both players stay within the theory for several more moves. } g6 12. Be2 Bg7 13. Na5 Qc7 14. c4 b6 $6 { This is a standard idea, but after studying this game I believe White’s queenside plans are just stronger than Black’s kingside counterplay. } ( { I would recommend } 14... e4 $5 15. f4 $6 ( { preferable is } 14... b6 14. O-O exf3 16. Bxf3 { with approximately level chances } ) 
Ng4 16. Bxg4 hxg4 17. O-O f5 { when Black had a good position in Mamedov – Korobov, Moscow 2011. } ) 
15. Nc6 Nb8 { Offering a favourable knight trade. } 16. Nb4 { This knight will be essential in White’s queenside plans, hence it should stay on the board. } ( { But not } 16. Nxb8 $6 Rxb8 17. O-O O-O 18. Rac1 ( { or } 18. b4 e4 ) 
a5 { (a key move, preventing b2-b4) } 19. b3 Nd7 { when Black had a comfortable position in Hajnal – Papp, Hungary 2008. } ) 
Nbd7 17. O-O O-O 18. Rac1 ( { PH: Inspired by modern computers, current players would consider something like } 18. g4 $5 { , when after } hxg4 19. fxg4 { the g4-pawn controls Black from carrying out the desirable ...f5 break. Black might try } e4 { , as the desirable } 19. f4 { would leave the g4-pawn hanging, but after } 19. gxh5 $14 { White is doing well. } ) 
Nc5 ( { If } 18... Nh7 { , trying to accelerate kingside play, then: } 19. Nc6 f5 ( 19... Nb8 $2 { no longer works, on account of } 20. Nxb8 Raxb8 21. b4 $1 Rfe8 22. c5 $16 { as White is breaking on the queenside far too quickly } ) 
20. b4 Rae8 21. Bd1 $5 { ± This is similar to the game; White’s chances are much better, as he has strong plans on both flanks. } ) 
 ( { Alternatively, trying to open lines with } 18... e4 { can be met by } 19. f4 $1 { since there is no time for } Ng4 $6 20. Bxg4 hxg4 { because of } 21. f5 $1 { with advantage. } ) 
19. Nc2 a5 { Preventing b2-b4. } ( 19... e4 { is met by } 20. f4 $1 { keeping the e4-square unavailable to Black’s knights, and threatening f4-f5 } ( 19... a5 19. Nd4 $5 { is also worth considering } ) 
Ng4 21. Bd4 Bxd4 22. Nxd4 Qe7 ( { but not } 22... f5 $2 23. b4 Nd7 24. Ne6 { and wins } ) 
23. Bxg4 hxg4 24. f5 $1 { ± with a strong attack. } ) 
 ( { If } 19... Rae8 20. b4 Ncd7 21. Na1 $5 { (planning Nb3 and c4-c5) } a5 $5 ( { if Black tries } 21... h4 22. Nb3 Nh5 { then } 23. c5 $1 { ± and the queenside collapses } ) 
22. bxa5 bxa5 23. Nc2 $14 { followed by Na3-b5. } ) 
20. Na3 $14 { Surprisingly, this advanced position has occurred in no fewer than five games (White scored 80%) . This is an indication of how typical, and how strong, this knight manoeuvre is. } Nh7 $6 ( { More accurate was } 20... h4 $5 21. Nb5 Qe7 22. Bd1 Nh5 23. b3 f5 24. a3 h3 $1 { allowing Black to continue fighting, though White’s position was still preferable in Goloshchapov – Anisimov, Russia 2006. } ) 
 ( { PH: Flores Rios suggests } 20... h4 { , but White can go for this pawn with } 21. Qe1 h3 22. g4 $1 { , and } e4 { can be met by } 23. f4 { when White keep things under control. } ) 
21. Nb5 Qe7 22. b3 f5 ( 22... e4 { does not work well after } 23. Bf4 $1 Be5 24. Bxe5 Qxe5 25. f4 $1 Qe7 26. f5 $16 { creating a kingside attack and isolating the e4-pawn. } ) 
23. Bd1 $1 { The right plan. } Bf6 ( { The try } 23... e4 $6 { is met by } 24. Bc2 exf3 25. gxf3 $1 { when only White benefits from the opening of new lines, for example } Rfe8 26. Rce1 Qd7 27. Kh1 { followed by Rg1 with a near decisive advantage. } ) 
 ( { Whereas the attempt } 23... f4 { , hoping to play 24...e4 , is met by } 24. Bxc5 $1 bxc5 25. Bc2 $16 { preventing } e5-e4 { and obtaining a big advantage due to the control of the light squares and Black’s bad bishop. } ) 
24. Bc2 $16 { The reader should pay close attention to how powerful White’s light-squared bishop is in this position. This bishop not only neutralizes Black’s plans on the kingside, but also creates the potential threat g2-g4 blowing up the f5-g6-h5 pawn barrier. } Rad8 25. Rce1 Ng5 26. a3 Nf7 $2 { The decisive mistake. } ( { A better defence was } 26... Ra8 { but after } 27. Nxd6 $5 ( { another option is } 26... Nf7 26. Kh1 { preparing the strong g2-g4 break } ) 
Nxf3 ( { or } 27... Qxd6 28. Bxg5 $18 ) 
28. gxf3 Qxd6 29. Kh1 $16 { Black is under serious pressure down the g-file. } ) 
27. b4 axb4 ( { Or } 27... Nd7 28. Na7 Ra8 29. Nc6 Qe8 30. g4 $1 { +- which is similar to the game. } ) 
28. axb4 Nd7 29. Na7 Ra8 ( 29... Qe8 30. Nc6 Ra8 { transposes to the game. } ) 
30. Nc6 Qe8 31. g4 $1 { +- Successfully breaking through Black’s kingside pawn structure. } Ra2 ( { If } 31... hxg4 32. fxg4 f4 33. Bxf4 $1 { winning a pawn because of the pin down the e-file. } ) 
32. gxf5 g5 { Black attempts to build a fortress, but the game is lost beyond repair. The rest is simple and somewhat irrelevant, hence I leave it without much comment. } 33. Qd1 Kh7 34. Bb1 Ra8 35. Kh1 Rg8 36. Rg1 Rg7 37. Rg2 Qc8 38. h4 $1 { Opening up the position! } gxh4 39. Rg6 ( { Better was } 39. Rxg7 $1 Kxg7 40. Rg1 Kf8 41. Qd2 { winning on the spot due to the threat of Bh6 . } ) 
Qa6 40. Reg1 Kh8 41. f4 Rh7 42. Qe2 h3 43. fxe5 dxe5 44. Bc1 Re8 45. Bb2 Qc8 46. Qe3 Qc7 47. Be4 Qd6 48. Bc1 Qf8 49. Qxh3 { Black’s position falls apart little by little. } Nd6 50. Bd3 Ra8 51. Bb2 Nf7 52. Qg3 Re8 53. Re1 Bg7 54. Re6 Bf6 55. Rg1 Rg7 56. Qh2 Ng5 57. Be2 Rh7 58. Rxg5 Bxg5 59. Nxe5 { The fortress is finally destroyed. } Rxe6 60. Nxd7+ Kg8 61. Nxf8 { Final remarks 1. Special attention should be played to the notes to Black’s moves 16 and 18. The essential element in trading those knights is whether Black will be able to play ...a6-a5 preventing b2-b4. If that is the case, then Black will generally do well since the queenside play loses most of its strength. 2. The bishop transfer Bd1-c2 followed by the kingside break g2-g4 is extremely powerful. 3. After analyzing this position one might be left with the impression that Black’s kingside play is not really going anywhere if White knows his way around. In the game this was true, but Black could certainly have improved his chances with the safer 14... e4 as suggested above. } 
 1-0

