 { Mauricio Flores Rios – Neuris Delgado, American Continental Championship 2009 Learning objective: This game is an interesting example of Black’s kingside attack against White’s passed central pawns. } 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 d6 6. f3 e5 7. Nb3 Be7 8. Be3 O-O 9. Qd2 Be6 10. Nd5 Bxd5 11. exd5 Nb8 { We have reached the thematic position. White will try to expand on the queenside with the usual c2-c4-c5. } 12. Be2 Nbd7 13. Na5 { Opening the way for b2-b4. } Qc7 14. c4 $5 ( { Similar is: } 14. O-O Bd8 $1 15. b4 ( { of course, returning with the knight would be inconsistent: } 15. Nb3 $6 Nb6 $1 16. Bxb6 Qxb6 17. Kh1 a5 $15 ) 
Qb8 $1 { Threatening ...Bxa5 destroying White’s queenside structure. } 16. Nb3 Bb6 $11 { Trading dark-squared bishops ensures a good position for Black, as in the game Cubas – Delgado Ramirez, Asuncion 2010. } ) 
Nh5 $5 { This ambitious move is typical, opening the way for ...f7-f5. The resulting position is objectively preferable for White, but rather complicated in a practical game. } ( { Simpler and somewhat preferable was } 14... Bd8 $5 15. b4 Qc8 16. Nb3 Bb6 ( 16... b6 $5 ) 
 { trading dark-squared bishops with level chances in Kernazhitsky – Chernyshov, Zalakaros 2005, since after } 17. c5 dxc5 18. Rc1 { then simply } c4 $1 { ⩱. } ) 
15. b4 $1 { A precise move order. } ( { Weaker is } 15. O-O Nf4 16. b4 ( { but not } 16. Bd1 $6 { because of } Bd8 $1 17. b4 Qc8 18. Nb3 Qxc4 ) 
Nxe2 17. Qxe2 f5 { with level chances. } ) 
f5 ( { White is ready to meet } 15... Nf4 { with } 16. Bd1 $1 { . It is a good idea to keep this bishop on the board, for example after } f5 17. Rc1 Rf7 18. O-O { ± Black is in trouble, due to the threat of Bb3 followed by c4-c5. } ) 
16. O-O Rf6 $6 { This plan is interesting but somewhat too slow. } ( { An alternative was } 16... Rae8 17. Rac1 b6 18. Nc6 Bh4 { with a complicated position. } ) 
17. Rfd1 Rg6 18. Rac1 { Preparing a c4-c5 break. } b6 { During the game I thought at first that this was an excellent decision, as I had to decide between passively going back to b3, or placing my knight on c6. I was unsure if the knight on c6 would be useful at all, but fortunately for me, I found a tactical resource to refute Black’s plan. } ( { An alternative was } 18... Nf4 19. Bxf4 exf4 20. Kh1 $16 { when Black is likely to lose a pawn on the kingside, but there is some counterplay associated with the control of the dark squares. } ) 
 ( { If Black simply waits with } 18... Rf8 $6 { then } 19. c5 $1 { opens the queenside favourably. } ) 
19. Nc6 $1 { The key to this move becomes clear in the next two moves. } ( { Incorrect is } 19. Nb3 $2 { Since c4-c5 is no longer strong, and Black may develop a strong kingside initiative, such as: } Qd8 ( { Another option is: } 19. Nc6 Rf8 $5 ) 
20. Kh1 ( { Or } 20. Bd3 e4 $1 21. fxe4 f4 22. Bd4 f3 { with attacking chances for Black. } ) 
Bh4 21. Bd3 e4 22. fxe4 f4 { There is nothing better than giving up the exchange with: } 23. Bxf4 ( { Bad is } 23. Bd4 $2 f3 $1 { with a winning attack, since } 24. gxf3 $6 { loses to } Ng3 $3 25. hxg3 Bxg3 { followed by ...Qh4 deciding the game. } ) 
Nxf4 24. Qxf4 Bg5 25. Qf2 Bxc1 26. Rxc1 Ne5 $15 ) 
Bh4 20. Bd3 Rf8 ( { The alternative was } 20... f4 21. Bf2 Bxf2 22. Qxf2 $16 { followed by a2-a4-a5 with very good prospects. } ) 
21. Qc2 $1 { Unexpectedly winning a pawn, since f5 cannot be protected. } e4 $1 { A good decision: Black has to give up a pawn, but does it in the form of a typical sacrifice obtaining some compensation. } ( { But not } 21... Rgf6 $2 22. Ne7 { . This is the point of White’s 19th move, which was probably missed by Black: } Kh8 23. Nxf5 { Winning even more material. } ) 
22. fxe4 $2 { Certainly not the right way to make use of the advantage, as it allows Black good compensation. } ( 22. Be2 $1 { was accurate, forcing material gain under better circumstances: } f4 ( { if } 22. fxe4 exf3 $2 23. Bxf3 Bg5 24. Bxg5 Rxg5 25. h4 $1 { +- } ) 
23. Qxe4 $1 { I missed this move, when White should be winning, though Black retains some practical chances. If } fxe3 24. Qxh4 Nf4 { , then the intermezzo } 25. Ne7 { saves the day: } Kh8 26. Nxg6 { or } Kf7 26. Qxf4 { . } ) 
f4 { The reader should be familiar with this type of pawn sacrifice; Black gives up a pawn, but gains space on the kingside, as well as the e5-square for a knight. Chances are balanced again. } 23. Bf2 ( { Or } 23. Bd4 f3 { with a dangerous attack. } ) 
f3 24. g3 Ne5 $1 { A precise move order. } ( { Weaker is: } 24... Nxg3 $6 25. Bxg3 $1 ( { but not } 24... Ne5 24. hxg3 $6 Ne5 26. Nxe5 dxe5 27. Kf1 Bxg3 28. Bxg3 Rxg3 { with reasonable compensation for the piece } ) 
Ne5 ( { but not } 25... Bxg3 $2 26. Ne7 $18 ) 
26. Nxe5 dxe5 27. Kf2 { White should be winning with precise play, though Black still has some practical chances with } Qd8 $5 { . For example } 28. c5 $2 ( { better is } 27... Ne5 27. Rg1 $16 ) 
Bxg3 29. hxg3 Rxg3 $1 30. Kxg3 Qg5 $11 { forcing a perpetual check, since } 31. Kh2 $2 ( 30... Ne5 30. Kf2 $11 ) 
 { loses to } Rf4 { with forced mate. } ) 
25. Nxe5 dxe5 { The reader should examine this critical position carefully. Black has employed a pawn sacrifice which is typical of this structure, and now White’s position seems to be on the verge of losing, on account of potential piece sacrifices on g3, followed by a mating attack. When I reached this position however, I was somewhat optimistic, since I had calculated: } 26. Kf1 $1 { Which is apparently the only move, and the beginning of an interesting idea. I decided to bring the king to a safer place, even at the cost of material, to be able subsequently to push the c- and d-pawns decisively. } ( { Instead, attempting to consolidate the kingside with } 26. Bf1 $2 { fails to } Qd8 27. c5 Nxg3 $1 { with a decisive attack: } 28. hxg3 Bxg3 29. Bxg3 Rxg3 30. Kf2 ( { or } 30. Kh1 f2 { with forced checkmate } ) 
