Bg5 { Preparing the ...Qd7-h3 invasion. } ( { Of course not } 26... Qd7 $2 27. gxh4 { . } ) 
 ( { The piece sacrifice } 26... Nxg3 $5 { is still possible: } 27. hxg3 { and now } Bxg3 ( { or } 27... Rxg3 $5 { with unclear position } ) 
 { leads to a draw after the forced line: } 28. Bxg3 Rxg3 29. Qh2 $1 Rg2 30. Qh3 Qd8 $1 31. Rc2 Qg5 32. Rxg2 fxg2 33. Kg1 Qf4 34. Kxg2 Rf6 { Black’s attack could be dangerous, therefore White should choose: } 35. Qc8 $1 Kf7 36. Qd7 Kf8 ( { but not } 36... Kg6 $2 37. Rg1 $1 { +- } ) 
37. Rf1 Rg6 38. Kh1 Rh6 39. Kg2 Rg6 { A very nice perpetual check. } ) 
27. Ke1 $1 { A prophylactic move, freeing the f1-square in order to reply to 27... Qd7 with 28. Bf1 preventing the deadly invasion on h3. } ( { Instead, the natural } 27. Rb1 $2 { allows } Qc8 $1 { when the entry on } 28. h3 { is decisive, for example: } 28. Ke1 Qh3 29. Bg1 f2 $1 { Opening the f-file decisively. } 30. Bxf2 Rgf6 31. Bg1 Rf1 $1 32. Bxf1 Qxf1 ) 
Qd7 $5 ( { Or } 27... Bxc1 28. Rxc1 { is similar to the game. } ) 
28. Bf1 { Necessary and effective, keeping Black’s offensive under control. } Nf6 ( { If } 28... Bxc1 29. Rxc1 Nf6 30. c5 { transposes to the note to the next move. } ) 
29. c5 $1 { Black’s initiative has been neutralized, and now the pawns start marching. } ( { Again } 29. Rb1 $2 { , keeping the exchange, gives Black some initiative after: } Ng4 $5 ) 
Ng4 { Creating the threats ...Nxh2, ...Ne3 or ...Be3. } ( { If } 29... Bxc1 30. Rxc1 { White has more than enough compensation for the exchange: } Ng4 ( { or } 30... Rg4 31. Bd3 ) 
31. c6 Qd6 32. Qb3 $1 { ± Black’s threats have come to a stop, while the passed pawns are very strong. } ) 
30. d6 { One more step toward promotion; the threat is c5-c6-c7 nearly winning. In addition, this move creates the possible check Qb3, protecting the e3-square against ...Be3 or ...Ne3. } ( { PH: } 30. c6 $1 { would have won an important tempo and given White a technically winning position. } ) 
bxc5 ( { Necessary was } 30... Nxf2 $1 31. Kxf2 Rh6 32. h4 Qg4 { with a very unclear position. } ) 
 ( { The capture } 30... Nxh2 $2 { is way too slow: } 31. c6 Qc8 32. c7 { followed by d6-d7 winning. } ) 
 ( { And of course not: } 30... Be3 $2 31. Bxe3 Nxe3 32. Qb3 Kh8 33. Qxe3 $18 ) 
31. bxc5 Bxc1 $2 { The decisive mistake. } ( { Again, necessary was } 31... Nxf2 $1 32. Kxf2 Rh6 { with an unclear position. } ) 
32. c6 $1 { +- A very important intermediate move, after which White is just winning. } Qc8 ( { Or: } 32... Qd8 33. c7 Qd7 34. Rxc1 $18 ) 
 ( { If } 32... Be3 33. cxd7 Bxf2 34. Qxf2 Nxf2 35. Bc4 $1 Kh8 36. Kxf2 { the endgame is winning with the transfer Bf7-e8. } ) 
33. Qxc1 { White is an exchange down, but the two pawns on the 6th rank decide the game. } Nxf2 ( { Again there is no time for } 33... Nxh2 { because of: } 34. c7 $1 Qd7 35. Ba6 $18 ) 
34. Kxf2 Rh6 35. h3 $1 { Black’s threats are completely neutralized, and the rest of the game is just a tactical exhibition. } Qb8 $5 { An interesting resource during time trouble, searching for counterplay with 36...Qb6. } ( 35... Rxh3 $2 { fails to: } 36. d7 Rh2 37. Kg1 Qc7 38. Kxh2 $18 ) 
36. Qc4+ Kh8 37. Qb5 { Blocking the queen’s invasion. } Qc8 38. Qc5 { An X-ray attack against the rook on f8. } Rg8 39. d7 $1 { This rather paradoxical pawn advance decides the game nicely. Typically, one should push pawns onto squares of the opposite colour to one’s bishop, but here we can make an exception for tactical reasons. } ( 39. c7 $5 { +- would be more conceptual. } ) 
Qc7 40. d8=Q { This is the motive behind the previous move; Black is now unable to prevent the promotion of the c6-pawn. } Rxd8 41. Rxd8+ Qxd8 42. c7 Qc8 43. Qe7 $1 { Threatening Qd8 with checkmate. } Rf6 ( { If } 43... Re6 { White has the nice variation } 44. Qd8 Re8 45. Qxe8 $1 Qxe8 46. Ba6 { followed by promotion and winning with an extra piece. } ) 
44. Ba6 $1 ( { A neat final blow. Black resigned since his pieces are overloaded in the defence of the 8th rank: } 44. Ba6 Qg8 45. c8=Q $11 ( { Or } 44. Ba6 Rxa6 45. Qd8 ) 
 ( { Or } 44. Ba6 Qxa6 45. Qd8 ) 
 { Final remarks 1. A critical factor during the opening phase was whether Black would be able to exchange pieces, reducing White’s queenside potential. The comments to moves 14 and 15 deserve special attention. 2. Despite some tactical inaccuracies, Black’s kingside potential proved dangerous throughout the game. 3. The reader should remember the typical pawn sacrifice ...e5-e4 followed by ...f5-f4. 4. The most exciting moment in this game is probably when White plays Kf1-e1 giving up the exchange in order to secure his king and continue advancing his central passed pawns. } ) 

 1-0

